`Resume the session and immediately run /compact so the stale results are compressed rather than dropped.`,
`Fork the earlier session so both the pre-refactor and post-refactor views stay available.`], correct:0, rationale:`When prior tool results are stale, injecting a structured summary into a fresh session is more reliable than resuming with outdated file contents. Resumption is the right choice when prior context is mostly still valid.`},
{domain:0, official:false, q:`You resume a named session with --resume after changing three files that Claude analysed earlier. The rest of the codebase is untouched. What should you do first?`, options:[
`Tell the session which specific files changed so it re-analyses only those, rather than re-exploring everything.`,
`Ask it to re-read the entire module to be safe, since you cannot know what it still remembers.`,
`Clear the context and paste the three diffs as the only starting material.`,
`Start a new session, since any file change invalidates a resumed one.`], correct:0, rationale:`Informing a resumed session about specific file changes enables targeted re-analysis and preserves the valid parts of the prior exploration. Full re-exploration wastes context; the prior analysis is not globally invalid here.`},
{domain:0, official:false, q:`Your team wants to compare two refactoring strategies for the same module, both starting from the codebase analysis Claude just completed. What is the appropriate mechanism?`, options:[
`fork_session, to create independent branches from the shared analysis baseline.`,
`Two --resume calls against the same session name, run one after the other.`,
`A subagent per strategy, each re-exploring the codebase from scratch.`,
`A single session that evaluates both strategies in one conversation, to keep the comparison consistent.`], correct:0, rationale:`fork_session exists precisely to explore divergent approaches from a shared baseline. Sequential resumes contaminate the second branch with the first, and re-exploration wastes the analysis you already paid for.`},
{domain:0, official:false, q:`Policy states that refunds above 500 dollars must go to a human. In testing, the agent occasionally processes a 780 dollar refund itself despite a clear system-prompt rule. What is the correct fix?`, options:[
`A hook that intercepts the outgoing tool call, blocks it above the threshold, and redirects to the escalation workflow.`,
